## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
MOFs   
Microwave absorption   
Self-assembly   
Porous structure   
Magnetic particles
Herein, we construct two metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) composite nanoflowers (NFs) with anisotropic bi­ nary assembled structures by a facile one-pot method. Different from the two-step epitaxial growth method, the anisotropic assembly of the binary MOFs (MOF-5 and MIL-88B) in the mixed system can be realized without preparing the template MOFs. The ions coordinated self-assembling behaviors during the formation of the threedimensional (3D) structure are clarified. The absorbing mechanisms of the derivative absorber are elucidated. The results indicate that the special hierarchical assembled structure endows absorbers with rich heterogeneous interfaces, porous structure, suitable impedance matching and complementary loss mechanisms. The optimized absorbers FeCoZn $@\mathrm{C}$ NFs and $\mathrm{FeNiZn}@\mathsf C$ NFs exhibit outstanding microwave absorption performance. Only with a matching thickness of $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ , the effective absorbing bandwidth (EAB, reflection loss (RL) $)<-10~\mathrm{dB}\rangle$ of the absorber covers $6.0\mathrm{GHz}$ (12–18 GHz, the entire $\mathtt{K u}$ band). The minimum reflection loss $(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}})$ reaches $-53.1$ dB (loss of electromagnetic waves above $99.999\%$ ). This study might shed light on the preparation of highperformance microwave absorbers with sophisticated structures.
## 1. Introduction
The fast-developing science and technology has brought great con­ venience to human life, but also caused serious electromagnetic pollu­ tion [1,2]. High-efficient and stable microwave absorbing materials (MAMs) can not only resist the increasing electromagnetic pollution, but also have been widely used in the protection of electronic components, military and other fields [3,4]. Therefore, MAMs with good physical and chemical properties are constantly being developed. Typically, carbon nanotubes [5] (CNTs) and carbon fibers [6] (CFs) are representative of one-dimensional (1D) materials. Two-dimensional (2D) materials and composite materials such as graphene [7], molybdenum sulfide [8] $(\mathbf{MoS}_{2})$ and transition metal carbides, nitrides or carbonitrides [9] (MXene) are also emerging in endlessly. Among them, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are highly sought after in electromagnetic materials due to their rich chemical composition and unique three-dimensional (3D) structures [4,10,11]. Through simple carbonization technologies, the conversion of MOFs precursors into high-performance absorbers (carbonized derivatives) can be realized [4]. Typical absorber pre­ cursors include ZIF-67 [12], prussian blue [13], UIO-66 [14], MIL-88B [15], MIL-125 [16], etc. [10], owing to their tunable chemical compo­ sition, large specific surface area and hierarchical pore structure, they often exhibit desirable microwave absorption characteristics [11].
However, traditional MOFs-derived absorbers are only formed by the conversion of a single MOFs precursor. The structure and chemical composition are relatively simple. Besides, they often exhibit difficultto-adjust impedance matching and monotonic loss mechanisms [17,18]. In order to develop high-efficient absorbers with more rich structures, richer loss mechanisms, and more widely used applications, multiple assembled MOFs-derived MAMs came into being [19–22]. Researchers integrate different types of MOFs crystals into one, such as ZIF-67@ZIF-8 [19], UIO-66@UIO-67 [20], MIL-125@ZIF-67 [21], UIO66/DUT-52@MIL-88B [22], etc. [23]. The assembled MOFs-derived absorbers possess a richer chemical composition, complementary loss mechanisms and easy-to-adjust impedance matching, thus exhibiting superior microwave absorption characteristics [22].
The common method for the preparation of multiple assembled MOFs is the epitaxial growth method. Specifically, the host MOFs is first synthesized as a template, and then the integration of two or even three MOFs crystals is realized by surface modification and epitaxial growth technologies. Their morphology always displayed isotropic, such as core–shell structure [19] and sandwich structure [23], and is relatively simple. It is worth mentioning that when the unit cell parameters of the two MOFs are similar and have a certain lattice matching, an anisotropic assembled structure can be constructed [21,22], retaining the intrinsic characteristics of various types of MOFs, such as octagonal nanoflower [22] and rod [23]. However, due to the distinct unit cell parameters and lattice mismatch of MOFs, the preparation of such highly complex assembled MOFs is relatively challenging and has not been explored [17,18,22].
Herein, we have realized the construction of two 3D assembled MOFs with high-structural complexity only through a one-step method (Fig. 1). The unique ions coordinated self-assembling behaviors were revealed, the morphology was effectively controlled by controlling the ions ratio, and the microwave absorption mechanisms of the derived absorbers were clarified. The results show that 3D magnetic porous absorbers ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@\mathsf C$ $\mathbf{\tilde{M}}=\mathbf{Co}$ or Ni) MOFs) possessed positive imped­ ance matching and rich loss mechanism, thus displaying remarkable microwave absorption characteristics.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Raw materials
Iron(III) acetylacetonate $\mathrm{(Fe(C_{5}H_{7}O_{2})_{3}}$ , $98\%$ ), cobalt(III) acetyla­ cetonate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ , $98\%$ , nickel(II) acetylacetonate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{2},$ $98\%$ , Zinc(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ ) and tereph­ thalic acid (BDC, $99\%$ ) were purchased from Adamas-beta. Poly­ vinylpyrrolidone (PVP, K30) was supplied by Sigma-Aldrich. N, Ndimethylformamide (DMF, ${>}99.5\%\mathrm{\}$ and ethanol absolute $(>99.7\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. All chemical re­ agents used were of analytical and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of FeMZn $\boldsymbol{{\cal M}}={\cal C o}$ or Ni) MOFs
Typically, $38.4\mathrm{mg}$ of BDC and $650\mathrm{mg}$ of PVP were first dissolved in a mixed solution of $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and ethanol (DMF: ethanol $=35~\mathrm{mL}$ : $15~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Subsequently, $60~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ , $240~\mathrm{mg}$ of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{2})_{3}$ (mass ratio $\mathrm{Fe}{:}\mathsf{C o}=4{:}1_{.}$ ) and $92.8\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ were dissolved in the above mixed solution. The mixed solution was placed in a $100\mathrm{mL}$ polytetrafluoroethylene lined reactor and reacted at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
After the product was cooled to room temperature, it was washed three times with DMF and twice with absolute ethanol, and octagonal FeCoZn MOFs was obtained after drying. In order to investigate the effect of ions ratio on its morphology, the content of other components was kept un­ changed, and the mass ratio of $\mathrm{Fe(C_{5}H_{7}O_{2})_{3}}$ to $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ was regu­ lated. When Fe:Co was 6:1, flower-like FeCoZn MOFs was obtained. When Fe:Co was 2:3, cubic FeCoZn MOFs was resulted. Similarly, Co $(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ was replaced with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{2})_{2}$ to prepare FeNiZn MOFs. When the mass ratio of $\mathrm{Fe(C_{5}H_{7}O_{2})_{3}}$ to FeNiZn MOFs (Fe:Ni) was 6:1, polygonal FeNiZn MOFs was obtained. When $\mathrm{Fe}{:}\mathrm{Ni}=4{:}1$ , octagonal FeNiZn MOFs was obtained. When $\mathrm{Fe}{:}\mathrm{Ni}=2{:}3$ , cubic FeNiZn MOFs was obtained.
## 2.3. Synthesis of MOF-5
$38.4\mathrm{mg}$ of BDC was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixed solution of DMF and ethanol (DMF: ethanol $=35\mathrm{mL}{:}15\mathrm{mL}$ . Subsequently, $240\mathrm{mg}$ of Fe $(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ was dissolved in the above mixed solution and fully dissolved with the aid of ultrasound. The mixed solution was placed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ polytetrafluoroethylene lined reactor and reacted at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the product was cooled to room temperature, it was washed with DMF and absolute ethanol, and dried to obtain MOF-5. For comparison, keeping the other components unchanged, $60~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2})_{3}$ , 60 mg of $\mathrm{Ni(C_{5}H_{7}O_{2})_{2}}$ and $240~\mathrm{mg}$ of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{2})_{3}$ were added to the re­ action system. The products obtained after the reaction were named CoMOF-5, Ni-MOF-5 and Fe-MOF-5.
## 2.4. Synthesis of MIL-88B
First, $38.4\mathrm{mg}$ of BDC was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixed solution of DMF and ethanol (DMF: ethanol $=35\mathrm{mL}$ : $15\mathrm{mL}$ ). Subsequently, $240\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe(C_{5}H_{7}O_{2})_{3}}$ was added to the above mixed solution, which was fully dissolved with the aid of ultrasound. The mixed solution was placed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ polytetrafluoroethylene lined reactor and reacted at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was cooled to room temperature, washed with DMF and absolute ethanol, and dried to obtain MIL-88B.
## 2.5. Synthesis of FeMZn@C $\boldsymbol{{\cal M}}={\cal C o}$ or Ni) NFs
$_{0.5g}$ FeMZn MOFs was placed in a quartz boat and kept at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ in a vacuum tube furnace. Subsequently, it was heated to $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for carbonization for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at a heating rate of $3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ , and the resulted products were named $\mathtt{F e C o Z n}@\mathsf{C}$ NFs or FeNiZn@C NFs.
## 2.6. Characterization
The morphology of the optimized samples was observed by field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM, Verios G4, FEI) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, TALOS-F200X, FEI). Raman spectroscopy was carried out using laser confocal Raman spectroscopy (WITec Alpha300R) with a laser excitation of $532~\mathrm{nm}$ . X-ray diffrac­ tometer (XRD, Thermo Scientific 7000, Thermo Fisher Scientific) was acquired recording the diffraction peaks of the samples from $10^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ . Infrared spectrometer (FTIR, Tensor 27, Bruker) was utilized to analyze the functional groups in absorbers. The specific surface area and pore size distribution of samples were measured by the BET (Brunauer
Emmet-Teller) method through the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption instru­ ment (Tristar 3020, Micromeritics). The carbonization process was performed in the tube furnace (KMTF-1100–30-220, Kemi). The samples were heated with solid paraffin and then mixed thoroughly to make cylindrical samples with inner and outer diameter of $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ and 7.00 mm. The electromagnetic parameters of the samples were measured in the vector network analyzer (N5227, Agilent) using the coaxial method.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology regulation
In order to explore the ion-coordinated self-assembling processes, the influence of ion ratio on the result of morphology was investigated. As shown in Fig. 2a-l, by changing the ratio of Fe ions to Co ions or Ni ions, the morphology control of the MOFs was successfully achieved.
When the Fe:Co ratio was 6:1, FeCoZn MOFs exhibited a regular nanoflower-like structure (Fig. 2a and b). When Fe:Co ratio was reduced to 4:1, FeCoZn MOFs displayed a defined octagonal nanoflower struc­ ture (Fig. 2c and d). When the Fe:Co ratio was 2:3, the flower-like FeCoZn MOFs disappeared and was replaced by cubic FeCoZn MOFs (Fig. 2e and f). Similarly, for FeNiZn MOFs, when Fe:Ni was 6:1, it appeared as polygonal NFs (Figure g and h). When Fe:Ni was 4:1, the morphology changed to regular octagonal NFs (Fig. 2i and j). Compared with the corresponding octagonal FeCoZn MOFs, the morphology was uniform and flat. When the ratio of Fe:Ni was 2:3, it was similar to FeCoZn MOFs and exhibited a cubic structure. The above-mentioned transformation behaviors of the morphology could be attributed to the changes in the specific proportion of the MIL-88B and MOF-5 structures in the binary assembled structure along with the ion ratios. When the concentration of Fe ions was reduced, the specific proportion of the structure corresponding to MIL-88B (the corner of the flower-like structure) gradually decreased, and finally changed to the structure of MOF-5 (cubic structure). It was worth noting that in addition to Fe ions, Co ions and Ni ions also played a vital role in coordinating the formation of the binary assembled structure. As shown in Fig. S1a-d, when Fe ions were not present in the reaction system, Co-MOF-5 and Ni-MOF-5 exhibited a cubic or disordered structure. In addition, when the reac­ tion system lacked Co or Ni ions, the Fe-MOF-5 only displayed a cubic structure instead of an octagonal flower-like structure. The results indicated that the production of the binary assembled nanoflower structure was the result of the coordinated action of various metal ions.
In order to further clarify the mechanism of ions coordinated selfassembly, the effect of reaction times on the self-assembly results was investigated. The specific coordinated assembling behaviors were illustrated in Fig. 3a. The generation of the binary assembled nanoflower structure was based on the structure of MOF-5. As the reaction pro­ gressed, the assembled structure tuned to cubic MOF-5 (Fig. 3b), inwardly concave cubic MOF-5 (Fig. 3c), MOF-5 with certain edges and corners (Fig. 3d-e), MOF-5 $@$ MIL-88B with clear binary assembly structure (Fig. 3f) and final octagonal FeCoZn MOFs (Fig. 3g). The re­ sults implied that the binary assembled structure could be constructed in one step under the coordination of multiple ions, and the specific pro­ cesses were similar to the anisotropic assembly behavior of two-step epitaxial growth [22].
The 3D assembled structures of FeMZn MOFs were characterized by SEM and TEM. The results were shown in Fig. 4a1-d6 and Fig. ${\bf S}2\mathrm{a_{1}}{-\bf d_{6}}$ . Interestingly, the 3D structure of FeMZn MOFs could be characterized from three representative angles: Fig. 4a1, Fig. 4b1 and Fig. 4c1, dis­ playing tetragonal (Fig. $4\mathbf{a}2$ and a3 as well as Fig. $\mathrm{{\s2a_{1}}}$ and $\mathrm{a}_{2})$ , hex­ agonal (Fig. $4\mathsf{b}2$ and b3 as well as Fig. $\mathbf{S2b}_{1}$ and $\left.\mathbf{b}_{2}\right|$ ) and heptagonal (Figure c2 and c3 as well as Fig. $\mathbf{S}2\mathbf{c}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{c}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\sigma}}$ ) NFs, respectively, showing a unique binary assembled structure. The energy dispersive spectrum (EDS)-mapping, as shown in Fig. 4d1-d6 and Fig. $S2d_{1^{-}}d_{6}$ , further revealed the composition of binary assembly. The evenly distributed C and O elements corresponded to the organic ligands in MOFs, and the Fe elements were concentrated outside the assembly to form angular MIL88B. Zn elements were concentrated in the middle of MOFs, corre­ sponding to round and square MOF-5. Ni or Co elements were evenly distributed on the whole assembly, proving that they participated in the process of ion coordinated self-assembling processes. Specifically, the XRD and FTIR results of FeMZn MOFs and its derived absorbers were revealed in Fig. 4e and f. The concentrated appearance of characteristic peaks of MOF-5 and MIL-88B confirmed the formation of binary assembled structure. The $\mathtt{C=}0$ bond of BDC at $1683~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ moved to­ wards the low wavenumber $(1589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , implying that the successful coordination between metal ions and organic ligands in FeMZn MOFs [24].
Similarly, for FeMZn MOFs-derived absorbers $({\mathrm{FeMZn}}@\mathbf{C}\ {\mathrm{NFs}})$ , the apparent morphology and internal structure were characterized (Fig. 5ar and Fig. S3a-r). As exhibited in Fig. 5a-f and Fig. S3a-f, FeMZn MOFs possessed good thermal stability and maintain structural integrity after high temperature carbonization. CNTs could be observed on the FeNiZn@C NFs, showing rich 3D structures (Fig. 5g and h). The highresolution TEM (HR-TEM) images of regions I and II exhibited lattice stripes of $0.19\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.20\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (1 0 2) crystal facet of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (JCPDS No. 79-2205) and the (101) crystal facet of Ni (JCPDS No. 89-7129) or the (1 1 0) crystal facet of FeNi alloy (JCPDS No. 37-
0474) (Fig. 5i and j). Three obvious diffraction rings appeared in the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) photos from inside to outside ascribed to (1 0 0), (1 0 2) and (1 1 0) crystal facets of Ni (JCPDS No. 89- 7129) (Fig. 5k) and (1 1 1), (2 0 0) and (22 0) crystal facets of Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806) (Fig. S3r), confirming the existence of Ni and Co nano­ particles. The concentration of C, O, Zn, Fe, Co and Ni indicated that there were abundant heterogeneous interfaces in the absorbers, which was conducive to the interface polarization effect (Fig. 5l-q and Fig. S3gn) [25]. Moreover, the lattice fringes with a spacing of $0.21\mathrm{nm}$ could be observed from the HR-TEM images outside FeCoZn@C NFs, corre­ sponding to the (1 1 1) crystal facet of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ (JCPDS NO. 52-0512). Based on the above XRD and FTIR test results, the derived absorbers were mainly composed of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ (JCPDS No. 79-2205), $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ (JCPDS No. 52- 0512), FeCo (JCPDS No. 49-1568), FeNi (JCPDS No. 37-0474), Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806) and Ni (JCPDS No. 89-7129). Additionally, there should be a small amount of oxides of Fe, Co and Ni in good accordance with the XPS test results (Fig. S4 and Fig. 5r). According to the highresolution XPS energy spectrum, there were characteristic peaks of C–C (284.8 eV), C–O (285.7 eV) and $\mathtt{C=}0$ $289.0\mathrm{eV})$ , indicating that there were oxygen defects in C components. It was conducive to the generation of polarizations [26]. At the same time, there were obvious characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}\left(707.1\right.$ and $719.5\mathrm{eV},$ ), $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (711.1 and 724.8 eV), $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ (713.5 and $730.5~\mathrm{eV})$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ (782.9 and $797.5\mathrm{eV}.$ ), ${\mathrm{Ni}^{0}}$ (853.1 and $872.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ (855.9 and $861.4\mathrm{eV})$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ (1021.9 and 1045.0 eV), belonging to the elemental substances of Fe, Co and Ni and their oxides as well as ZnO in consistent with the above characterizations [25–28].
## 3.2. Physical properties
Thermal stability was important to the practical application of MAMs. As shown in Fig. S5, under $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ conditions, the two absorbers displayed no obvious thermal decomposition before $316.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $373.5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , indicating that they possessed good thermal stability. The mass loss $12\%$ and $14\%$ ) was caused by the oxidation of the elemental metal and the decomposition of the carbon component. Raman spectroscopy was utilized to characterize the degree of graphitization and defects of absorbers. As illustrated in Fig. 6a, the curve appeared three distinct characteristic peaks, corresponding to D $(1339.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , G (1585.3 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ) and 2D $(2908.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) peaks. Among them, the intensity ratio of the D peak and the G peak $\mathrm{(I_{D}/I_{G})}$ represented the defect or the degree of graphitization of the sample [29]. The results demonstrated that $\mathtt{F e C o Z n}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs and FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs exhibited similar $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values, 0.98 and 0.99, respectively (Table 1). In addition, the appearance of 2D peaks manifested that they had a deep degree of graphitization. For the MAMs, defects were conducive to the generation of polarization, and a certain degree of graphitization was advantageous to the improve impedance matching [29]. Furthermore, the presence of magnetic components endowed the absorber with a certain magnetic loss capability [19]. As revealed in Fig. 6b, $\operatorname{FeCoZn}@\mathsf{C}$ NFs and ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs displayed a certain degree of paramagnetism. The specific saturation magnetization $\left(\mathbf{M}_{s}\right)$ and coercivity $\mathrm{(H_{c})}$ were $30.53\mathrm{emu}/8@399.56$ Oe and $11.39\mathrm{\emu/\}$ $g(\underline{{a}}604.78\$ Oe (Table 1) limited by the degree of crystallinity, size and content of magnetic nanoparticles [26]. The specific surface area and porous structure of absorbers were the key factors that determine the microwave absorption performance. The two sets of absorbers were characterized by a nitrogen adsorption–desorption instrument, and the results were displayed in Fig. 6c and d and Table 1. The specific surface area, average adsorption pore volume $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{BJH}})$ and average adsorption pore size $\mathrm{(D_{BJH})}$ of $\mathtt{F e C o Z n}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs and ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@\mathsf{C}$ NFs were $53.76\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\$ $\mathbf{g}@0.11~\mathbf{cm}^{3}/\mathbf{g}@11.62~\mathrm{nm}$ and $59.03~\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{g}@0.14~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}@12.38~\mathrm{nm}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , respectively. The pore size distribution covered micropores $(\sim2~\mathrm{nm})$ , mesopores $\scriptstyle2-50\ n\ m\}$ and macropores $(>50\mathrm{nm})$ ). It could be attributed to the pores retained after carbonization of MOFs, the mesopore struc­ ture of CNTs, and the pores and defects introduced during carbonization, in line with SEM and TEM results. Previous studies had shown that the large specific surface area and pore volume could expand the effective area of absorbers for microwaves [25]. The existence of the hierarchical pore structures would introduce additional air and effective interface, further enhancing the loss of the electromagnetic waves [30]. Besides, the existence of the porous structure was also conducive to optimizing the impedance matching characteristics of absorbers, providing basic conditions for dissipation [26].
## 3.3. Microwave absorbing properties
The effects of low $(40\%)$ , medium $(47.5\%)$ and high $(50\%)$ filler loadings on ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs were measured by coaxial method. The reflection loss (RL) performance of ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs was calculated, and the test samples were named FeMZn@C NFs-L, FeMZn@C NFs-M and
FeMZn@C NFs-H. RL values could be calculated by the following for­ mula [14]
$$
\mathrm{RL}=20l g\bigg|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-1}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+1}\bigg|
$$
$$
\mathrm{Z_{in}}=\mathrm{Z_{0}}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}}{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}t a n h\bigg[j\bigg(\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\bigg)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\bigg]
$$
among them, $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $Z_{0}$ were the normalized input impedance and the impedance of free space; $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{r}}$ were the relative complex permittivity and relative negative permeability of MAMs; $f$ was the frequency of electromagnetic waves; c was the speed of light, and $d$ was the matching thickness of samples. The calculation results were illus­ trated in Fig. 7a-l. For FeCoZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-L, when the matching thickness was $4.7\ \mathrm{mm}$ , the minimum reflection loss $(\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}})$ reached $-46.1$ dB at $5.9\mathrm{GHz}$ , and the effective absorption bandwidth (EAB) covered 2 GHz $(4.9\mathrm{-}6.9~\mathrm{GHz})$ (Fig. 7a and b). After increasing the filler loading, the microwave absorption performance of FeCoZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-M was improved. At $6.9\mathrm{GHz}$ , the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ reached $-51.2$ dB, and EAB was 2.5 GHz (5.8–8.3 GHz) corresponding to a matching thickness of $3.4~\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 7c and d). In addition, only with a matching thickness of $1.4\mathrm{mm}$ , FeCoZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs
H achieved an $\ensuremath\mathrm{{RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ of 29.4 dB (at $17.0\ \mathrm{GHz})$ , and EAB covered 3.5 GHz (14.5–18 GHz) (Fig. 7e and f). For FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs, FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFsL, it displayed poor microwave absorption performance. With a matching thickness of $5\mathrm{mm}$ , EAB was only 1.3 GHz (16.7–18 GHz), and the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ was $-15.5$ dB (at 17.9 GHz) (Fig. $_{78}$ and h). After increasing the amount of filler, FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-M also exhibited enhanced micro­ wave absorption characteristics. At $5.9\ \mathrm{GHz},$ the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ reached $-53.1$ dB, and EAB covered $3.4\:\mathrm{GHz}$ , corresponding to a matching thickness of $2.9\ \mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 7i and j). Additionally, when the matching thickness was reduced to $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ , EAB could reach 6 GHz (12–18 GHz, the entire Ku band) while maintaining excellent $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ $(-33.2~\mathrm{dB})$ . Continue to in­ crease the amount of filler, the improvement of microwave absorption capacity was not obvious. For FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-H, at a matching thickness of $1.9\mathrm{mm}$ , the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ reached $-51.1$ dB (at $13.6\mathrm{GHz}\mathrm{,}$ , and EAB covered $4.8\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (11.4–16.2 GHz) (Fig. 7k and l).
In order to compare the microwave absorption characteristics of the two absorbers, the RL and EAB under different thicknesses were compared, as shown in Fig. 8a and b. Obviously, FeCoZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-M and ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs-M displayed more positive microwave absorption per­ formance. In comparison, $\mathrm{FeNiZn}@\mathbf{C}\:\mathrm{NFs-N}.$ obtained a wider EAB while maintaining similar RL performance to FeCoZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-M. This might be due to the larger specific surface area and richer 3D structure of ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs [22]. In order to further explore the reasons, the impedance matching performance and attenuation constant (α) of samples were studied. The relationship between the matching thickness $\mathrm{(t_{m})}$ , RL as well as relative impedance matching $(|\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{Z}}}_{\mathrm{in}}/\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{Z}}}_{0}|)$ and frequency of ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathrm{C}}$ NFs was jointly analyzed. It was observed that the RL curves all shifted to the low frequency region with the increase of the matching thickness (Fig. 8c and d). This phenomenon could be explained by the quarter-wavelength matching model [31].
$$
{\mathrm{t}}_{\mathrm{m}}={\frac{n c}{4f_{m}{\sqrt{\left|{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{r}}\right|\left|{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{\mathrm{r}}\right|}}}}(n=1,3,5,\ldots)
$$
where $f_{\mathrm{m}}$ was the frequency corresponding to the $\ensuremath\mathrm{{RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ . In Fig. 8c and d, the red line was the theoretical matching thickness $(\mathrm{t_{m}}^{\mathrm{fit}})$ , and the black dot was the actual matching thickness $({\mathrm{t}}_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\mathrm{exp}}})$ . The perfect coincidence of ${\bf t}_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~fit~}}$ and ${\bf t}_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~exp}}$ represented that the reflection loss curves of absorbers conformed to the quarter-wavelength matching model. It was significant that the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ under different thicknesses were all achieved at the value of $\left|\mathrm{Zin}/\mathrm{Z}_{0}\right|$ closed to 1, implying that both $\mathtt{F e C o Z n}@\mathsf{C}$ NFs and ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs showed proper impedance matching [15]. Moreover, as shown in Fig. S6, FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs possessed a larger $\upalpha$ in a large fre­ quency range, suggesting that its internal loss behavior was stronger, thus exhibiting more outstanding microwave absorption performance [22]. In order to demonstrate the superiority of the materials, we compared their absorbing properties with other materials. As shown in Table S1, no matter in RL, EAB or thickness, ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs could exhibit relatively excellent performance.
The $\mu^{\ast}$ -frequency curves of all samples fluctuated above 1.0 (Fig. 9d), and the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ -frequency curves fluctuated above the 0 axis (Fig. 9e). Compared with the dielectric constant, the above values were smaller, implying that the dielectric loss effect was dominant [19]. In addition, $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ -frequency curves showed multiple sets of formants. This was the result of small size effect, surface effect and spin wave excitation [32]. Similarly, the magnetic loss tangent value of samples $(\tan\delta_{\mu}=\mu^{,}\prime\mu^{,})$ was also much less than $\begin{array}{r}{\tan\updelta_{\varepsilon}=\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime}}\end{array}$ value (Fig. 9F), proving the dominance of dielectric loss. It was worth noting that the $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon}=$ $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime}$ values of FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-L were less than zero at certain fre­ quencies. This was because under the incidence of high-frequency electromagnetic waves, FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs-L generated an induced mag­ netic field while forming a conductive network. Since the alternating electric field could induce and radiate the magnetic field, it leaded to the coupling between electricity $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon})$ and magnetism $(\tan\delta_{\mu})$ [30,34,35]. Specifically, when the conductivity of the composite was close to the percolation threshold, the eddy current effect began to shield the ma­ terial from the penetration of microwaves, thereby reducing its perme­ ability. The increase of conductivity leads to increased $\varepsilon$ values and decreased $\mu$ values even exhibited negative values [30,35]. The results indicated that the microwave absorption characteristics of ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs were the result of the combined effects of dielectric loss and mag­ netic loss.
Furthermore, the corresponding relationship between $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ ’ could better represent polarizations in absorbers. Their relationship could be expressed by the following formula [34]
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{'}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon/\eta\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{s}$ and $\varepsilon_{\infty}$ were static dielectric constant and relative dielectric constant. As shown in Fig. $9g$ and $\mathbf{h}$ , there were multiple Cole-Cole semicircles in the $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime}{}^{\prime}$ curves. Studies have shown that each semi­ circle implied a Debye dipole relaxation process [19]. Specifically, the
Debye relaxation process of ${\mathrm{FeMZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs-M could be attributed to following reasons: (1) The carbon components and the defects on the metal compounds could form dipoles, resulting in dipolar polarizations; (2) The abundant heterogeneous interfaces, such as ${\mathrm{C@Fe_{3}C_{:}}}$ , $\mathsf{C@C o}$ $\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{Ni}$ , and FeMZn@C NFs@paraffin could induce interface polarizations.
Moreover, the magnetic loss mechanism of absorbers could be expressed by the relationship between $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{1}$ and frequency $(f)$ [19]. Studies have shown that the magnetic loss in the range of 2–18 GHz was mainly caused by natural resonance, exchange resonance and eddy current effects [22]. As illustrated in Fig. 9i, when it was located at a high frequency $(2{\mathrm{-}}10~\mathrm{GHz})$ , the fluctuation of the of $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{1}$ curves with the change of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Sigma}}$ decreased, indicating that the magnetic loss mainly was derived from eddy current loss and exchange resonance [35]. At low frequency $(2{\mathrm{-}}10~\mathrm{GHz})$ , the value of $\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{1}$ would fluctuate signifi­ cantly with the change of $f$ . It could be attributed to natural resonance [6].
In summary, we summarized the possible microwave absorption mechanism in Fig. 10. In detail, the excellent microwave absorption ability of ${\mathrm{FeNiZn}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ NFs was mainly due to [4,26,35,36,37]: (1) The special 3D assembled structure equipped FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs with suitable impedance matching and strong attenuation characteristics; (2) The hierarchical pore structures could enlarge the effective interface and introduce additional air, thereby enhancing scatterings and microwave absorption; (3) A large number pore defects and metal nanoparticles in FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs could cause extensive dipole polarization effects; (4) Abundant heterogeneous interfaces, such as ${\mathrm{C@Fe_{3}C,}}$ , $\mathsf{C@C o}$ , $\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{Ni}$ and FeMZn@C NFs@paraffin could induce interfacial polarization effects; (5) Magnetic nanoparticles could endow absorbers with certain mag­ netic loss ability (eddy current loss and magnetic resonance); (6) Under high-frequency electromagnetic waves, dielectric and magnetic com­ ponents could form a 3D conductive network accompanied by electro­ magnetic coupling to realize the complementarity of loss mechanism.
## 4. Conclusion
In general, we have successfully constructed two novel binary assembled MOFs-derived absorbers. The mechanism of ions coordinated self-assembly was proposed. The morphology of MOFs was effective controled, and the microwave absorption mechanism was clarified. The results indicated that FeMZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs possessed well-matched impedance and exceptional electromagnetic attenuation performance. With only a matching thickness of $1.9\ \mathrm{mm}$ , the EAB of FeNiZn $@\mathsf{C}$ NFs covered the entire Ku band (12–18 GHz). At $8.9\mathrm{GHz}$ , the $\ensuremath\mathrm{{RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ exceeded $-53.1$ dB. This work might provide some guidance for the construction of elec­ tromagnetic composite materials with highly complex structures.
